  "mechanismSummary": "The nitrogen of 2-aminoethanol adds to a C=O of Boc₂O, and the tetrahedral intermediate expels a tert-butyl carbonate anion. That anion loses CO₂ to give tert-butoxide, which takes the proton from nitrogen. The product is a carbamate: its nitrogen lone pair is delocalised into the C=O, so it is no longer basic or nucleophilic. The OH group does not react, because nitrogen is the better nucleophile. The Boc group survives base, nucleophiles and hydrogenation, and comes off with acid.",
